deep.could_work_without_session_changes:
Everything it does works from passive reading of ~/.claude. It installs nothing into the observed session, so every feature could be rebuilt from passive JSONL reading. That includes:
- the live 'ongoing' indicator (heuristic plus 5-minute mtime staleness);
- per-subagent execution trees with tokens and duration;
- parallel-subagent detection (100 ms start window);
- Agent Teams reconstruction (teammate-message tags, teammate_spawned results, parentUuid chain across continuation files);
- context-window attribution and compaction phases;
- tool-call inspectors;
- todo state (~/.claude/todos);
- trigger-based error notifications;
- search;
- SSH remote viewing.

Claude Explorer could do better passively in five places:
- (1) Read agent-<id>.meta.json and workflows/<runId>/. claude-devtools ignores both, and they would give agent type and description before the Task result exists.
- (2) Recognize the 'Agent' tool name as well as 'Task'.
- (3) Link nested subagents to the subagent JSONL that holds the spawning tool_use, not to the main session.
- (4) Tail incrementally with byte offsets for the UI path too, so updates are deltas and not a full re-parse.
- (5) Send subagent-only file changes to the UI. In Electron mode the parent-file fingerprint can suppress them.

Not available from JSONL, and claude-devtools does not attempt any of it:
- true sub-second in-flight state between JSONL flushes (streaming tokens before a line is written);
- exact cost, since its costUsd is hard-coded to 0;
- hook-only signals such as permission prompts awaiting approval or PreToolUse timing before the tool_use line is written;
- process liveness. Its 'ongoing' state is a content heuristic plus a 5-minute mtime timeout, not a PID check.
